# What the knowledge graph must not ingest.
#
# Same syntax as .gitignore. graphify reads .gitignore first, then this file.
# Several entries below duplicate .gitignore deliberately: a graph can also be
# built from the PARENT directory, which is not a git repository at all, and in
# that scan there is no .gitignore to respect. An exclusion that only works from
# one working directory is not an exclusion.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cost, not correctness. 20,756 files / 545 MB. Left in, it is the corpus.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output. `out/` is regenerated by every `pnpm build` and the browser
# suites serve a snapshot of it; graphing it would graph the same source twice,
# once as authored and once as compiled.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
out/
.next/
.serve-snapshot/
playwright-report/
test-results/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The graph's own output. Ingesting it would let the previous run's report
# become an input to the next one, and GRAPH_REPORT.md names hundreds of
# identifiers -- a feedback loop that manufactures its own evidence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
graphify-out/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST.
#
# `registries/raw/extract/` holds 36 chunks DERIVED from the frozen blueprint,
# and it has already produced three distinct defect classes on this build by
# being mistaken for the source:
#
#   - the extractor's own wording printed as if it were the blueprint's prose
#   - the extractor's own line numbers printed as if they were the blueprint's
#   - a chunk pinning itself to a line, then that line cited as the source's
#
# Feeding it to the graph would relaunch all three as edges, and an edge tagged
# EXTRACTED would then assert the derived layer as read-from-source. The
# blueprint slices under ../blueprint-slices/ are the sanctioned derivation, and
# they carry a manifest that maps every line back to the original.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
registries/raw/extract/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS BUILD'S OWN WORKING NOTES, and this is a judgement rather than a saving.
#
# The graph's document half must be the SOURCE, not our commentary on it. These
# are plans, censuses and verification reports: derived, dated, and frequently
# wrong before they were right. Slice 6 alone found 22 wrong figures in briefs
# and 66 wrong locators in one plan document, all since corrected -- but a graph
# built over them would carry the corrected AND the superseded claims side by
# side, both tagged EXTRACTED, because to an extractor a confident sentence in a
# plan reads exactly like a confident sentence in the blueprint.
#
# That is the defect this build has already met three times in another costume:
# a derived layer mistaken for the source. The blueprint says what is true; the
# code says what was built; the notes say what we believed on a Tuesday.
#
# Excluded deliberately, not overlooked. Add them back only with a node type
# that marks them as commentary, never as source.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/superpowers/plans/
docs/superpowers/specs/
docs/census/
docs/process/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch. The probe convention is a leading dot AND a trailing process id --
# exact, never a prefix, so a genuine source file named `zz-probe.tsx` is still
# ingested. `tests/probe-paths.ts` carries the full account.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.zz-probe-*
*.tsbuildinfo
*.log
.DS_Store
